  "response": "## The connectivity picture first\n\nEight files cross the connectivity thresholds (p95: fan-in 9 / fan-out 6; p99: fan-in 10 / fan-out 11). They split cleanly into three different problems, and only one of them is a blast-radius problem:\n\n| File | fan-in | fan-out | Also | blast_radius (direct / transitive) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `src/utils.ts` | **18** | 1 | God File 424L | 0.46 (18 / 29) |\n| `src/index.ts` | 4 | **17** | God File 459L + 3 god functions | 0.28 (4 / 7) |\n| `src/esbuild/index.ts` | 1 | **11** | `runEsbuild` 237L | 0.25 (1 / 7) |\n| `src/plugin.ts` | 10 | 3 | `buildFinished` 103L | 0.45 (10 / 29) |\n| `src/log.ts` | 9 | 0 | — | 0.45 (9 / **30**) |\n| `src/rollup.ts` | **0** | 7 | `getRollupConfig` 122L | **0** |\n| `src/api-extractor.ts` | 1 | 6 | `rollupDtsFiles` 85L | 0.25 |\n| `test/utils.ts` | 9 | 0 | — | 0.32 |\n\nFan-in and fan-out are not the same lever. High fan-in means *your edits ripple outward*. High fan-out means *the file is hard to reason about, but nothing depends on it*. Only the first reduces blast radius across the codebase; the second reduces agent risk locally.\n\n## Triage order\n\n**1. `src/utils.ts` — split it, behind a re-export facade**\n\nfan-in 18 against a p95 cutoff of 9 and a p99 of 10 — twice the threshold, and the highest in the repo. 18 direct / 29 transitive importers. It's also the 424-line God File (crime_00083, 1.4× threshold). Crucially it has **no `large_function` finding at all** — it's a grab-bag of small helpers, which is exactly the file where a split is mechanical rather than surgical. Two real defects sit behind that fan-in and inherit the 0.46 blast radius: 7 sync I/O calls (crime_00012) and `jsoncParse` swallowing a parse failure and returning `{}` with no error binding at all (crime_00027) — 18 files consume that silent `{}`.\n\nDo it as a responsibility split that keeps `utils.ts` re-exporting, not a breaking move. With 18 importers, the import rewrite *is* the risk, and it's the part that buys you nothing.\n\n**2. `src/index.ts` — extract the nested god-function stack**\n\nfan-out 17, against a p95 of 6 and p99 of 11 — the worst in the repo, and 459 lines (crime_00087). It hosts the three largest functions found: `build` 293L (4.9×), the anonymous 274L body at 183–456, and `mainTasks` 194L (3.2×) — these are one nested stack, so a single extraction pass clears all three findings, plus `normalizeOptions` 96L, `startWatcher` 79L, `buildAll` 75L.\n\nThe reason this is second and not risky: **fan-in is only 4** (7 transitive). You are untangling a module that depends on everything while almost nothing depends on it — the ripple is bounded by construction. This is the best complexity-reduced-per-unit-of-risk trade available.\n\n**3. `src/esbuild/index.ts` — extract inside `runEsbuild`, don't restructure the module**\n\nfan-out 11, sitting exactly on the p99 cutoff. `runEsbuild` is 237 lines (4.0× threshold, crime_00065) and takes an options bag with **38 distinct property reads** (crime_00026) — the widest in the repo, and the single worst thing here for an agent trying to figure out which fields are required.\n\nThird rather than first because **fan-in is 1**: this buys comprehension, not decoupling. Nothing downstream gets safer.\n\n**4. `src/plugin.ts` — `buildFinished` only, leave the module boundary alone** (see below)\n\n**5. `src/cli-main.ts` — `main` (153L), on its own merits**\n\nHigh severity, but blast_radius 0.15 with 2 direct importers — the largest severity-to-ripple gap in the report. Worth doing, worth doing last of the scheduled work.\n\n## What I'd leave alone\n\n**`src/plugin.ts` (fan-in 10 — above p99).** The finding refutes itself: *\"9 of 10 importers take types only — a shared type module's coupling is compile-time, and being depended on is its job.\"* Splitting it would churn 10 files to remove coupling that doesn't exist at runtime. The one thing worth doing inside it is extracting `buildFinished` (103L, crime_00061) without touching the exported type surface.\n\n**`src/log.ts` (fan-in 9, fan-out 0, 30 transitive importers — the highest in the report).** It sits *at* the p95 cutoff of 9, not above it, and fan-out 0 makes it a pure leaf. A logger imported by everything is the correct shape, and with 30 transitive importers any restructuring here is the most disruptive change available for the least structural gain. Take the boolean rename (`onlyImportant`, `silent` → `is*`/`should*`, crime_00017) via codemod and stop there.\n\n**`src/rollup.ts` (fan-out 7, fan-in 0).** Every finding on this file reports `blast_radius: 0` — nobody imports it. It has genuine defects (`getRollupConfig` 122L, name/behaviour mismatch calling `setSilent`/`removeFiles` behind a `get*` prefix, `runRollup` swallowing its error) but zero ripple. Fix opportunistically when you're already in the file; don't schedule it.\n\n**`src/api-extractor.ts` (fan-out 6).** Exactly on the p95 cutoff — marginal by the detector's own numbers.\n\n**`test/utils.ts` (fan-in 9) and `schema.json` (362L).** Test scaffolding and a data file. `schema.json` has blast_radius 0 and \"split by responsibility\" isn't meaningful advice for a JSON schema.\n\n**The esbuild plugin god functions** — `postcss.ts` (`postcssPlugin` 140L / `setup` 126L) and `svelte.ts` (`sveltePlugin` 116L / `setup` 106L / anon 91L). All fan-in 1, blast 0.25, and each pair is one nested body counted twice. Self-contained plugins; defer.\n\n## Two caveats on this ordering\n\n**Every finding reports `churn: 0` and `recency: 0`.** No git-history signal reached this scan, so the ranking is purely structural. Normally you'd intersect fan-in with churn and refactor the *hot and connected* files first — a 424-line file nobody has touched in a year is a much weaker candidate than these numbers alone suggest. Worth re-running where history is available before committing to the order.\n\n**`test_gap` independently supports the same order.** `src/utils.ts` and `src/index.ts` both report `test_gap: 0` — you'd be refactoring with a net. Everything I deferred sits at `0.75`: `plugin.ts`, `log.ts`, `rollup.ts`, `cli-main.ts`, `api-extractor.ts`, `postcss.ts`. Two independent reasons to leave those last: low ripple benefit, and no tests to catch you if you're wrong.",
